= Background material from rings and modules <ch:rings-modules>

#source(13)Group representations are often studied as modules over the group
algebra (see Chapter~@ch:group-algebras), which is a finite dimensional algebra
in case the coefficients are taken from a field#idx("field", "of coefficients"),
and a Noetherian ring if integer or $p$-adic coefficients are used. Thus we
begin with a rather condensed summary of some general material on rings and
modules. Sources for further material related to Chapters~@ch:rings-modules
and~@ch:group-algebras are Alperin [@bib:Alperin1986], Curtis and Reiner
[@bib:Curtis1962, @bib:Curtis1981, @bib:Curtis1987], Feit [@bib:Feit1982] and
Landrock [@bib:Landrock1984]. We return for a deeper study of modules over a
finite dimensional algebra in Chapter~@ch:representations-algebras.

Throughout this chapter, $Lambda$ denotes an arbitrary ring with unit, and $M$
is a (left) $Lambda$-module.
